# Port pipeline: generated from the web project, never hand-edited
/[Aa]ssets/StreamingAssets/theblock-config.json

# Editor screenshots - throwaway captures, would otherwise eat the shared LFS quota
/Assets/Screenshots/
/Assets/Screenshots.meta

# District source GLBs - 40-70 MB each, authoritative copies live in
# ~/TheBlockSource/cities/. Free LFS is 1 GiB and shared with the original repo,
# so these stay out. first-one.glb is exempt: 240 KB and the ONLY copy anywhere.
/Assets/Models/City/procedural-city-*.glb
/Assets/Models/City/procedural-city-*.glb.meta

# WorldBuilder's generated output. The stripped district meshes are ~15 MB each and the
# alpha-clip materials point at textures inside those same gitignored .glbs - both are
# derived assets that every Build World rewrites from scratch and sweeps when stale, so
# committing them would cost LFS quota to store something that is never read.
/Assets/Meshes/Generated/
/Assets/Meshes/Generated.meta

# Road/water/sand materials and the road surface texture, all written by WorldBuilder
# and swept by it. Same reason as the generated meshes: reproducible, not authored.
/Assets/Materials/World/Generated/
/Assets/Materials/World/Generated.meta
/Assets/Materials/City/Cutout/
/Assets/Materials/City/Cutout.meta

# Character source FBX over 10 MB - raw Mixamo downloads, authoritative copies live in
# the game repo at source-assets/models/. Same LFS reasoning as the district GLBs.
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe.fbx
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe.fbx.meta
/[Aa]ssets/StreamingAssets/theblock-config.json.meta

# Jump animation FBX - 54 MB raw Mixamo download, same LFS reasoning as Joe.fbx.
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe_Jumping.fbx
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe_Jumping.fbx.meta
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe_Sprint.fbx
/Assets/Models/Characters/Joe_Sprint.fbx.meta

# U15's compressed district textures, and the material clones that point at them. Both are
# extracted from the gitignored district .glbs by The Block → Compress Textures, so they are
# derived assets with no source of their own here - and they are ~1 GB, which is the whole free
# LFS quota several times over.
/Assets/Textures/Generated/
/Assets/Textures/Generated.meta
/Assets/Materials/City/Compressed/
/Assets/Materials/City/Compressed.meta

# Lot-car paint materials - one per model per palette colour, written by WorldBuilder and
# swept by it. Same reasoning as the cutout materials: derived from the config's palette,
# rewritten by every Build World.
/Assets/Materials/LotCars/
/Assets/Materials/LotCars.meta

# U16's pedestrian pack - Chepatack "NPC Casual set 00", Unity Asset Store free tier, 505 MB
# (185 MB of FBX, 280 MB of .tif). The authoritative copy is the .unitypackage in
# ~/Library/Unity/Asset Store-5.x/Chepatack/, so this is a re-downloadable dependency and not
# a source asset. Free LFS is 1 GiB shared with the original repo - this alone would be half of it.
/Assets/npc_casual_set_00/
/Assets/npc_casual_set_00.meta

# Everything The Block → Build Pedestrians makes. The prefabs reference meshes and avatars inside
# the gitignored pack above, so committing them would commit dangling GUIDs; the tinted shirt
# materials are clones of the pack's own. One menu item rebuilds all of it.
/Assets/Prefabs/Npc/
/Assets/Prefabs/Npc.meta
/Assets/Materials/Npc/
/Assets/Materials/Npc.meta

# The baked NavMesh - 2.3 MB, written by Build World's navigation pass and swept by it. Derived
# from the gitignored district .glbs, so it has no source of its own here either.
/Assets/Navigation/
/Assets/Navigation.meta

# U17's traffic build output. The baked street graph and the lamp mesh/materials are derived from
# the config plus a raycast against the gitignored district .glbs, and Build World rewrites and
# sweeps all of it - the same reasoning as the NavMesh it is built beside.
/Assets/Traffic/
/Assets/Traffic.meta

# Everything The Block → Build Traffic Cars makes. The prefabs point at material clones that in
# turn point at textures inside the gitignored Textures/Generated, so committing them would commit
# dangling GUIDs; one menu item rebuilds both folders from the tracked car .glbs.
/Assets/Prefabs/Traffic/
/Assets/Prefabs/Traffic.meta

# U35h - the three street-prop prefabs. Same reason as the traffic cars: they point at material
# clones under Materials/City/Compressed that point into the gitignored Textures/Generated.
# The Block → Build Props (or Build World) rebuilds them from the tracked Models/Props/*.glb.
/Assets/Prefabs/Props/
/Assets/Prefabs/Props.meta
/Assets/Materials/Traffic/
/Assets/Materials/Traffic.meta

# The Asset Store officer's raw download - 459 MB of uncompressed .tga, and *.tga is LFS-tracked
# against a free 1 GiB shared with the original repo.
#
# SETTLED at U19e: it stays ignored permanently and NOTHING references it. `sips` wrote 1024² PNG
# twins of its 20 textures and its 2.3 MB FBX was copied out, so the whole officer is 24 MB in
# Assets/Models/Characters/Officer/ and that is what CopOfficerBuilder reads. Do not un-ignore this
# folder; re-run the conversion instead if the source is ever needed again. It can be deleted.
Assets/Police_officer/
Assets/Police_officer.meta

# Blender writes a .blend1 backup beside every save. It is a duplicate of the file next to it.
*.blend1

# Snell Roundhand, split out of macOS's SnellRoundhand.ttc for the Coca-Cola wordmark on the
# falafel stand's sign. Apple's licence, so it stays local - tools/build-falafel.py regenerates
# it from the system .ttc when it is missing.
/source-assets/fonts/

# U30b's performance sweep output - 44+ Player.log copies per run, one per configuration. They are
# EVIDENCE, not source: tools/perf-report.py turns them into the table that goes in the ledger, and
# the table is what is worth keeping. Re-runnable with tools/perf-sweep.sh.
/perf-sweep-out/
/perf-recheck-out/

# Written by Unity's Performance Testing package when it builds a development Player. Empty stubs
# (`{"MeasurementCount":-1}`), regenerated on every build, and they land in Assets/Resources so
# they would otherwise ship inside the game.
/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json
/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunInfo.json.meta
/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json
/Assets/Resources/PerformanceTestRunSettings.json.meta

# Generated preview of the kanban board - re-made by tools/kanban/render-html.mjs.
tools/kanban/board.html

# The FBX importer unpacks a model's embedded media into a sibling <model>.fbm folder at import
# time. Every PNG in there is byte-identical (md5) to the one in that character's own *Textures/
# folder, nothing anywhere references the .fbm copies, and the folder comes back on reimport - it
# was moved out of Assets/ and regenerated identically, GUIDs included, before this rule was
# written. So tracking it spent 320 MB of a free 1 GiB LFS quota - shared with the game repo - on a
# second copy of something the importer makes for free.
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*.fbm/
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*.fbm.meta

# Mixamo ships a specular/glossiness texture set beside every character. URP's Lit shader is
# metallic/smoothness and has no input for either, so not one of these 28 files is bound by any
# material - each was checked on its own, not inferred from the naming. 57 MB of the same quota.
#
# Scoped to Characters/ deliberately: a bare *_Specular.png would eventually swallow a texture that
# IS wired up. The Diffuse and Normal maps beside these are NOT here and must not be added - the six
# street characters resolve them by NAME through Models/Characters/People/Materials/*.mat, a binding
# that leaves no GUID trail and so reads as an orphan to any reference scan.
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Glossiness.png
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Glossiness.png.meta
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Specular.png
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Specular.png.meta
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Gloss.png
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Gloss.png.meta
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Opacity.png
/Assets/Models/Characters/**/*_Opacity.png.meta
